tion {:value "255939"} "Kayak Singles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "255967"} "Kayak Doubles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "255981"} "Kayak Fours, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "255990"} "Canadian Singles, 500 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "256002"} "Canadian Singles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "256014"} "Canadian Doubles, 500 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "256022"} "Canadian Doubles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "256039"} "Kayak Singles, 500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "256048"} "Kayak Doubles, 500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "256057"} "Kayak Fours, 500 metres, Women"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "255468"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "255607"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "255701"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "255809"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "256092"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "256226"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "256337"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "258763"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "2008517"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/162"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Kayak Doubles, 500 metres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "3 –  7 August 1992"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/674"} "Canal Olímpico de Cataluña, Castelldefels"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/255953"} "60 from 30 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "New Zealand’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10430"} "Ian Ferguson"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10436"} "Paul MacDonald"] " had won the men’s K-2 500 at the last two editions of the Olympic Games, but had been absent from the World Championship podium for the past three years. " [:a {:href "/athletes/9894"} "Kay Bluhm"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9915"} "Torsten Gutsche"] ", on the other hand, had been there every year, winning it for East Germany in 1989, then taking bronze in 1990, and then coming runner-up in 1991 for a unified Germany. The reigning World Champions were " [:a {:href "/athletes/9509"} "Juan José Román"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9510"} "Juan Sánchez"] ", while the Soviet Union’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9162"} "Sergey Kalesnik"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10652"} "Anatoly Tishchenko"] ", competing under the banner of the Unified Team for the 1992 Summer Olympics, had taken the title in 1991."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Kalesnik and Tishchenko posted an Olympic record time in the opening round, defeating Román and Sánchez in the process, while the New Zealanders were sent to the repêchage in Germany’s heat and advanced to the semi-finals only by being the fastest fourth-place finisher among the heats. The other heats were won by Americans " [:a {:href "/athletes/11090"} "Michael Harbold"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/11136"} "Peter Newton"] " and Poland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10475"} "Maciej Freimut"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10499"} "Wojciech Kurpiewski"] ", the 1989 World bronze medalists. In the first semi-final heat, the Italian duo of " [:a {:href "/athletes/10201"} "Antonio Rossi"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10177"} "Bruno Dreossi"] " not only defeated Kalesnik and Tishchenko, but bested their Olympic record and outperformed the Americans. Germany took the other heat over Poland, also getting the better of the Spaniards and helping to eliminate Ferguson and MacDonald. With the two-time defending Olympic champions out of the picture, the gold was guaranteed to go to a new nation, and it found its home around the necks of the deserving German team, who grabbed it over a second and a half before Poland nabbed silver ahead of Italy by a margin of only 0.16 seconds. Bluhm and Gutsche would go on to become double Olympic champions by winning the K-2 1000, while Italy won its first canoe sprint medal since 1960 (" [:a {:href "/athletes/10178"} "Pierpaolo Ferrazzi"] " had won the men’s K-1 Slalom earlier in the Games)."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Rep"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "144/147"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/9894"} "Kay Bluhm"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/9915"} "Torsten Gutsche"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "1:33.95 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "1:28.11 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "1:28.27 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "244/250"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10475"} "Maciej Freimut"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/10499"} "Wojciech Kurpiewski"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/POL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/POL.png"}] "POL"]] [:td {} "1:34.76 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "1:29.66 (2 h2)"] [:td {} "1:29.84 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "202/196"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10201"} "Antonio Rossi"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/10177"} "Bruno Dreossi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-centr